.TH DUMMYDIR 1 "2026-08-31" "DummyDir @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dummydir \- generate and manipulate a directory tree of files for testing purposes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dummydir
.I operation directory
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B dummydir
.I directory
.BI \-\-replay= replay-file
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dummydir
(DummyDir) generates and manipulates a directory tree of files for testing
purposes. It uses a deterministic, seedable pseudo-random number generator,
so a run can be reproduced exactly later from its recorded seed.
.PP
Every dummy directory maintains a manifest file,
.BR DummyDir.manifest ,
in its root, which tracks the files and subdirectories that
.B dummydir
created and is responsible for. Operations only ever add, modify, or remove
items recorded in this manifest; files and directories not created by
.B dummydir
are never touched, even if they live inside the target directory.
.PP
Every run is also logged to a replay file,
.BR DummyDir.replay ,
in the root of the target directory. This file records each operation and
the flags (including the random seed) it was invoked with, so the exact
sequence of operations can be reproduced later with
.BR \-\-replay .
.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.B add
Add new files to the dummy directory.
.TP
.B delete
Delete existing files from the dummy directory.
.TP
.B modify
Modify the contents of existing files.
.TP
.B rename
Rename existing files.
.TP
.B move
Move existing files to a different directory.
.TP
.B dadd
Add new subdirectories.
.TP
.B drename
Rename existing subdirectories.
.TP
.B dmove
Move existing subdirectories to a different parent directory.
.TP
.B ddelete
Delete existing subdirectories, along with everything inside them.
.TP
.B clean
Remove every file and directory this tool has created.
.TP
.B verify
Check that every file on disk matches what the manifest recorded.
.TP
.B rebuild
Rebuild the manifest from what is actually present on disk.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-size= value
Total number of bytes the operation should affect. Accepts K/M/G/T/P/E
suffixes (e.g.\& 500M, 2.5G), a percentage of the relevant total (e.g.\&
25%), or a range to pick randomly from (e.g.\& 1k\-10m). Shorthand:
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BI \-\-count= value
Total number of items (files or directories) the operation should affect.
Accepts the same percentage and range rules as
.BR \-\-size .
Shorthand:
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BI \-\-fileseed= value
A numerical value that acts as a seed for file add and modify operations.
Files with identical seeds will have identical data. Ranges may be used.
.TP
.BI \-\-filesize= value
For
.BR add ,
the size of each new file. For
.BR modify ,
how much of each file's content to change. Same value rules as
.BR \-\-size .
.TP
.BI \-\-filetype= type
For
.B add
only:
.BR random ", " binary ", " sparse ", or " text .
Default:
.BR random .
.TP
.BI \-\-modifytype= type
For
.B modify
only:
.BR append ", " truncate ", " overwrite ", " chop ", " insert ", or " random .
Default:
.BR random .
.TP
.BI \-\-maxdepth= N
For
.B dadd
and
.B dmove
only: how many levels deep the directory structure is allowed to go.
Default: 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads= N
Number of worker threads to use for file operations. 0 or 1 disables
threading. Default: the number of hardware threads available.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed= hex
Seed for the pseudo-random number generator, as a hex string. If not
given, a random seed is generated and recorded so the run can be
reproduced later.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print details about every individual error, conflict, or discrepancy
found, instead of just a summary count. Shorthand:
.BR \-v .
.TP
.BI \-\-replay= file
Execute the operations, in order, from the replay file at
.IR file ,
against
.IR directory .
The file location is required.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, an unrecognized operation or flag, or a missing/invalid
directory path, exception thrown, or files did not pass validation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B DummyDir.manifest
Located in the root of the target directory. Tracks every file and
directory
.B dummydir
has created there, along with each file's size and MD5 hash.
.TP
.B DummyDir.replay
Located in the root of the target directory. A log of every operation
performed against it, in the exact form needed to reproduce the run with
.BR \-\-replay .
Reproducing a run means feeding this file to a
.I different
target directory; see
.B \-\-replay
above.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Add 500 megabytes of new files, each between 10 and 20 megabytes:
.B dummydir add ./mydir \-\-size=500M \-\-filesize=10M\-20M
.TP
Delete 25% of the files currently in the directory:
.B dummydir delete ./mydir \-\-count=25%
.TP
Move 5 random subdirectories, keeping the tree to 3 levels deep or less:
.B dummydir dmove ./mydir \-\-count=5 \-\-maxdepth=3
.TP
Check every file against the manifest, listing any problems found:
.B dummydir verify ./mydir \-\-verbose
.TP
Rebuild the manifest from what is actually present on disk:
.B dummydir rebuild ./mydir
.TP
Reproduce a recorded sequence of operations in a new, empty directory:
.B dummydir ./newdir \-\-replay=./mydir/DummyDir.replay
.SH NOTES
Every operation, once completed, reports how many bytes and how many
items were affected, along with the resulting MD5 hash of the manifest
file. Two runs performed with the same sequence of operations and the
same recorded seeds will always produce manifests with an identical hash,
which makes
.B dummydir
suitable for reproducible test fixtures and regression baselines.
.PP
Numeric flags can be presented as a single number (eg 101), range of
numbers (eg 101-150), percentage (50%), or range of percentages
(25%-50%). A letter can be placed after the number to indicate
a base 2 multiplier (eg 10k = 10240).
.PP
.B dummydir
will only directly manipulate and delete files that it creates
though directory operations will affect all files in it. It is generally
recommended that foreign files be kept out of the target directory.
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Chris Bartle.
